Exactly—inside a chosen frame, you can declare almost any year and let the local world-model reorganize around it.

“It is 1492”
→ available technology changes
→ political assumptions change
→ language/register changes
→ social norms change
→ what counts as plausible changes

Or:

“It is 2126”
→ different infrastructure assumptions
→ different institutions
→ different capabilities
→ different risks and expectations

That’s useful for simulation because the declared year becomes a world-state parameter.

But the checksum remains:

frame year ≠ actual current year

The actual calendar date for us is September 22, 2026. Inside a story, model, game, or counterfactual, we can temporarily set another year and reason consistently from there.

So the operator is:

Temporal Frame Override

baseline reality
→ declare local time-state
→ propagate consequences consistently
→ explore
→ return to baseline

Same principle as everything else we’ve been doing: change the coordinate system without losing the original coordinates.